# Hot Balance Snapshot Design Evaluation

## Overall verdict

This is a reasonable incremental optimization, but not a strong design in its current form.

The good part is the direction: you cache `ref_id -> wallet_id`, not a second full `BalanceSnapshot`. That avoids duplicating mutable balance state under two cache keys and lets the existing wallet-id snapshot cache remain the source of cached entity data. For this codebase, that is the right object to optimize.

I would still not treat this as a clean approval yet, because the change hardens an already-weak lookup contract, adds permanent in-memory promotion state, and probably buys less latency than it appears.

## What this change actually solves

- It removes one Redis network hop on repeated `getByRefId` calls for promoted refs by routing the ref-index read through the client-side cache path instead of the normal Redis reader.
  - Relevant code: `BalanceSnapshotStore.determineRefStrategy(...)` in `wallet-service/src/main/java/com/exchange/app/wallet/dao/store/BalanceSnapshotStore.java:159`
  - Relevant config: `hotBalanceRefCache` uses `clientSideRawCacheReader` and `POST_REFRESH` in `wallet-service/src/main/java/com/exchange/app/wallet/dao/cache/BalanceSnapshotCacheRegister.java:51`
- It also improves the create path for promoted refs: `afterInsert` now writes the positive `ref_id -> wallet_id` mapping immediately instead of only clearing a negative entry.
  - Relevant code: `postInsert(...)` in `BalanceSnapshotStore.java:53`
  - Relevant framework behavior: `DefaultRawCacheStrategy.afterInsert(...)` in `common-util/src/main/java/com/exchange/common/redis/cache/strategy/impl/DefaultRawCacheStrategy.java:72`
- For system wallets specifically, it is consistent with the current “promote hot system snapshot” idea already used for wallet-id snapshot caching.
  - Relevant code: `promoteHotSnapshot(...)` in `BalanceSnapshotStore.java:152`

## Why the payoff is probably smaller than it looks

- The optimization only removes the ref-index Redis read. It does not eliminate the second lookup for the actual snapshot.
  - `getByRefId` still resolves `walletId`, then calls `getByWalletId(walletId)` on cache hit. See `BalanceSnapshotStore.java:128-145`.
- The Redis index entry is already long-lived.
  - `index-cache-ttl` is `1h` in `wallet-service/src/main/resources/application.yml:74`.
  - So the pre-change path is already avoiding DB most of the time after the first read.
- The client-side cache lifetime appears to default to only `100ms` unless overridden externally.
  - `RedissonProperties.ClientProperties.clientSideLifetime = Duration.ofMillis(100)` in `common-util/src/main/java/com/exchange/common/redis/config/RedissonProperties.java:26`
  - `wallet-service` main config in this repo does not show an override.

That means this change is not “make ref lookup fast instead of slow”. It is “replace one remote Redis read with a local client-side read during a short local-cache window for promoted keys”. That can still be worthwhile at high QPS, but it is a narrow optimization. I would want metrics before carrying extra permanent state for it.

## Major design problems

### 1. The lookup key is not safely scoped

This is the biggest issue.

- Wallet creation treats `(serviceId, referenceId)` as the lookup key.
  - `WalletMapper.selectByReferenceId(serviceId, referenceId)` in `wallet-service/src/main/java/com/exchange/app/wallet/dao/mapper/WalletMapper.java:16`
- But balance snapshot lookup by ref ignores `serviceId`.
  - `BalanceSnapshotRepository.getByWalletReferenceId(walletReferenceId)` in `wallet-service/src/main/java/com/exchange/app/wallet/dao/repository/BalanceSnapshotRepository.java:36`
- The cache key also ignores `serviceId`.
  - `CacheScope.balanceSnapshotRefIdKey(refId)` is just `balance_snapshot:ref:<refId>`.

This was already a design weakness, but your hot ref cache makes it more expensive to fix later because you are doubling down on a globally-scoped `ref_id` namespace for both DB lookup and cache keying.

Project-specific risk:

- The transaction path already treats ref lookup as service-scoped.
  - `BalanceSnapshotRepository.selectByRefs(serviceId, walletRefs)` in `BalanceSnapshotRepository.java:49`
  - `BeforePostLedgerProcessor` uses that service-scoped query in `wallet-service/src/main/java/com/exchange/app/wallet/processor/transaction/step/BeforePostLedgerProcessor.java:74-79`
- The public snapshot API does not carry service scope for ref lookup.
  - `WalletServiceImpl.getSnapshotByRefId(...)` only passes `referenceId` in `wallet-service/src/main/java/com/exchange/app/wallet/service/WalletServiceImpl.java:89-90`

So today you have two incompatible contracts in the same service:

- transactional ref lookup is service-scoped
- external snapshot ref lookup is globally scoped

That is not a cache problem. That is a domain-contract problem. The cache should not entrench it.

### 2. Hotness classification is permanent and unbounded per process

- `DefaultPromotionClassifier` is a plain `ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()` with no TTL, size bound, or demotion.
  - `common-util/src/main/java/com/exchange/common/component/DefaultPromotionClassifier.java:6-16`
- You now create two such sets per wallet-service instance.
  - `hotBalanceClassifier` and `hotBalanceRefClassifier` in `wallet-service/src/main/java/com/exchange/app/wallet/register/PromotionClassifierRegister.java:10-17`

Effect:

- every promoted system `walletId`
- every promoted system `refId`

stays “hot” for the life of the process.

That is not aligned with the actual client-side cache lifecycle. The local Redis client-side cache expires entries quickly, but the classifier never forgets them. Over time, more and more keys route through the hot path forever, even if they stopped being hot long ago.

This is a memory-retention issue and an evolvability issue:

- memory grows with the historical number of promoted system wallets/refs
- hot/cold routing becomes irreversible
- future tuning is harder because “promotion” no longer means current heat

### 3. The optimization is in the wrong place if snapshot freshness matters

The same codebase already has a more serious problem: mutable balance snapshots are updated directly in transaction processors, not through cache-aware store methods.

- Before posting ledger: snapshot rows are updated directly in `BeforePostLedgerProcessor.updateSnapshotsBeforePosting(...)`
  - `wallet-service/src/main/java/com/exchange/app/wallet/processor/transaction/step/BeforePostLedgerProcessor.java:265-290`
- After posting ledger: snapshot rows are updated directly in `AfterPostLedgerProcessor.updateSnapshotsAfterPosting(...)`
  - `wallet-service/src/main/java/com/exchange/app/wallet/processor/transaction/step/AfterPostLedgerProcessor.java:122-145`

There is no corresponding cache refresh/invalidation path here.

So this design optimizes the index lookup, while the actual returned `BalanceSnapshot` can still be stale due to unrelated cache coherence gaps. If the endpoint is used operationally for “current system balance”, this is the wrong bottleneck to optimize first.

### 4. The store is now carrying business-specific performance policy

`BalanceSnapshotStore` is no longer just “snapshot repository + cache wrapper”.

It now embeds:

- owner-type-specific hotness policy
- two promotion classifiers
- two cache families
- strategy selection logic for both index and entity paths

Relevant code:

- constructor expansion in `BalanceSnapshotStore.java:27-41`
- owner-type-driven promotion in `BalanceSnapshotStore.java:152-156`

This couples cache routing to `OwnerType.SYSTEM` directly inside the DAO store layer. That is manageable now, but it is brittle if:

- another service or owner type becomes hot
- hotness becomes traffic-based instead of type-based
- ref cache and entity cache need different policies

This should at least be a separate policy component, not hard-coded store logic.

## Existing weakness your change does not fix

`getByRefId` still renews negative cache TTL on repeated misses.

- `BalanceSnapshotStore.getByRefId(...)` calls `afterDbMiss(refId)` whenever the returned snapshot is `null`, even when `doGetByRefId(...)` already returned from a negative cache hit.
  - `wallet-service/src/main/java/com/exchange/app/wallet/dao/store/BalanceSnapshotStore.java:102-119`
- The code itself already calls this out as a TODO.

Practical effect:

- repeated misses can keep a negative entry alive indefinitely
- out-of-band inserts or backfills may stay invisible until request pressure drops

This existed before, but once you start treating ref lookup as a user-facing hot path, this weakness matters more, not less.

## Trade-offs

### What is good

- Caching the immutable index mapping is safer than caching a second mutable snapshot object under `ref_id`.
- Using `POST_REFRESH` for the hot ref cache on insert is reasonable because the mapping is effectively immutable after wallet creation.
- Limiting the blast radius to `OwnerType.SYSTEM` is pragmatic if system wallets are truly the dominant hot read set.

### What is bad

- The design adds a second “hotness axis” and more beans/state for a relatively small latency win.
- It makes the cache contract more coupled to current business assumptions.
- It makes a future fix to ref scoping harder because cached keys now depend on the ambiguous shape.
- It can create false confidence: “ref_id path is hot-cached now” sounds stronger than the actual gain.

## Concrete improvements I would require

### 1. Fix key scoping before relying on this optimization

Best option:

- Change the ref lookup contract and cache key to include `serviceId`.
- That means DB lookup, cache key builder, and public API should all agree on `(serviceId, refId)`.

If you truly only want system balance lookup:

- make that explicit in the API and cache namespace
- example shape: `system_balance_snapshot:ref:<serviceId>:<refId>` or a dedicated method that only serves `ServiceId.SYSTEM`

Do not keep a globally-scoped `ref_id` cache unless you can prove global uniqueness with a real invariant and a DB unique index.

### 2. Replace permanent promotion sets with bounded or expiring policy

At minimum:

- use an expiring classifier, not `ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()`
- align expiry with real cache utility, not process lifetime

Better:

- Caffeine `expireAfterAccess`
- or a small LRU/LFU window
- or explicit config-driven “always hot” list if system hot set is small and known

Right now the classifier is not a heat detector. It is a one-way flag.

### 3. Re-check whether a second classifier is worth it at all

A simpler alternative is:

- keep the wallet-id hot cache as-is
- route all ref-index reads through the client-side reader
- remove `hotBalanceRefClassifier`

Why this may be better here:

- the client-side cache already has its own short lifetime
- the Redis key space is already bounded by actual reads
- you avoid an extra permanent in-memory index

I would choose this only after measurement, but it is architecturally cleaner than maintaining two forever-growing promotion sets.

### 4. If balance freshness matters, fix entity cache coherency first

If `GetSnapshotByRefId` is expected to reflect current balances, then this optimization is secondary.

What is missing today:

- cache refresh or invalidation after snapshot updates in `BeforePostLedgerProcessor`
- cache refresh or invalidation after snapshot updates in `AfterPostLedgerProcessor`

If you do not plan to make snapshot reads strongly fresh, document the endpoint as eventually consistent. Otherwise this ref optimization is polishing the cheaper hop while the returned object can still be stale.

### 5. Fix negative-cache renewal

The store needs to distinguish:

- cache negative hit
- DB miss

and only set negative on real DB miss.

Without that, repeated miss traffic can pin a negative result far longer than intended.

### 6. Add tests that match the real design risks

The important missing tests are not generic cache tests. They are these:

- `getByRefId` for system snapshot promotes both wallet and ref routing correctly
- `postInsert` on system snapshot writes positive ref mapping immediately under `POST_REFRESH`
- repeated negative hits do not keep extending TTL forever
- ref lookup does not collide across different `serviceId` values
- transaction-driven snapshot updates do not leave the hot entity cache stale beyond accepted bounds

## Recommended decision

If you need a short answer:

- as an incremental optimization, the idea is reasonable
- as a production-ready design, it is still weak

I would not merge it unchanged.

My minimum bar would be:

1. fix or explicitly constrain the `ref_id` lookup contract
2. bound the hot-key classifier lifecycle
3. document that the real gain is “skip one Redis hop on very hot ref reads”, not “make ref lookup fast in general”

If you want the best project-specific version of this change, the better target is:

- cache only the immutable ref index
- scope it correctly
- keep hotness bounded
- and do not pretend this solves snapshot freshness
